Based on my analysis, the PR change is a security *improvement*, not a flaw. The base code fetched all `MailRule` and `MailAccount` objects matching the query with only a global permission check (`MailRule.objects.filter(name__icontains=query)`), which would expose other users' owned mail objects (CWE-639/IDOR). The PR replaces this with `get_objects_for_user_owner_aware`, which scopes results to objects owned by the user, unowned objects, or objects with explicit guardian view perms — matching the documented object-level permission model ("By default objects are only visible and editable by their owner... objects can have no owner which makes them visible to any user"). The added test confirms the filtering works. No authorization flaw is introduced by this change.

